  "reasoning": "The patient presents with sneezing, a sore throat, excessive mucus in the nose and throat, a swollen neck, and pain when swallowing. These symptoms closely mirror reference [1], which describes nearly identical complaints (sneezing, gunk/mucus in the nose and throat, sore throat, swollen neck) and correctly diagnoses them as a common cold. Additionally, the patient explicitly states they have a 'bad cold,' which directly points to this diagnosis. While 'allergy' can cause sneezing and a sore throat (as seen in reference [20]), it typically involves watery/itchy eyes or lip/eye swelling and lacks the prominent mucus production and painful neck swelling characteristic of an upper respiratory infection. The combination of significant mucus, neck swelling, pain on swallowing, and the explicit mention of a cold firmly establishes 'common cold' as the correct diagnosis.",
